    editor: { pageTitle: 'Keyfob designer - SLOU', title: 'Design your keyfob', lead: 'Pick a shape, the leather and thread colour, add your initials - and look at it from every side.', hint: 'Drag to rotate, scroll to zoom', shape: 'Shape', shapes: { rect: 'Rectangle', arch: 'Arch', point: 'Point' }, leather: 'Leather colour', thread: 'Thread colour', ring: 'Ring', rings: { silver: 'Silver', brass: 'Brass', black: 'Black', gold: 'Gold' }, initials: 'Initials', initialsPh: 'e.g. EM', colors: { tan: 'Natural', cognac: 'Cognac', brown: 'Brown', black: 'Black', burgundy: 'Burgundy', olive: 'Olive', cream: 'Cream', white: 'White', red: 'Red' }, product: 'Item', products: { keyfob: 'Keyfob', card: 'Card wallet' }, card: { title: 'Design your card wallet', lead: 'Four pieces of veg-tan leather, saddle-stitched by hand: a pocket on each side and one more in the middle.', patternTitle: 'Card wallet No. 1', recommend: 'Recommendations', how: 'Cut the inner piece twice, the front and back pieces once each. Glue only the margin up to the stitch line and stack them: front, inner, inner, back (the top stays open). Punch the holes through all four at once.', body: 'Inner piece × 2', outer: 'Outer piece × 2', viewFront: 'Front', viewBack: 'Back', finished: 'The finished card wallet', specs: 'Specifications', pocketLine: 'Pocket edge', holes: 'Holes: 3.85 mm pricking iron', threadSize: '0.55 mm recommended', ends: 'Thread ends: 2 stitches sewn twice at each', vegtan: 'veg-tan', pieces: 'Pieces', pocketColor: 'Pocket colour', window: 'Window: cut in the back pocket only', process: { title: 'Work process', steps: [['Template tracing and cutting', 'The templates are traced onto the leather using an awl, ensuring accurate outlines before cutting. Each piece is then carefully cut to shape.'], ['Flesh side treatment', 'The flesh side of the leather is treated with Tokonole to smooth and refine the surface.'], ['Edge preparation before assembly', 'The edges of areas that will become difficult to access after assembly are beveled using an edge beveler. These edges are then burnished with a mixture of Tokonole and water to achieve a smooth, compact finish. As a final step, the edges can be finished with a heated iron and leather wax for a smoother, more refined appearance, although this step is optional.'], ['Gluing and initial assembly', 'The leather pieces are assembled in stages: pieces No. 1 and No. 2 are glued together, followed by pieces No. 3 and No. 4. Ecostick 1816 B contact adhesive is applied in thin layers to ensure an even bond.', 'Once both sections are prepared, the front and back assemblies are glued together, with particular attention paid to keeping the edges as precisely aligned as possible.', 'After assembly, the glued areas are tapped with a hammer or secured using edge clamps to ensure firm contact between the surfaces. The adhesive is then left to dry completely. For the best results, the leather pieces should remain pressed together throughout the drying process.'], ['Edge sanding and refinement', 'After the adhesive has fully cured, the assembled edges are sanded progressively, beginning with 400-grit sandpaper, followed by 800 grit and finally 2000 grit.', 'Tokonole and water are applied between sanding stages to help smooth and consolidate the leather fibres. Before the final finishing stage, the edges are refined once more with an edge beveler to remove sharp corners and create a clean, even profile.'], ['Stitching line and hole preparation', 'The stitching line is marked approximately 2.8 mm from the edge using wing dividers. Consistent spacing is essential for a neat and uniform saddle stitch.', 'Stitching holes are punched using 3.85 mm pricking irons, following the marked line and maintaining consistent alignment along the entire seam.'], ['Hand saddle stitching', 'The pieces are saddle-stitched entirely by hand using 0.55 mm thread and John James Saddlers Harness Needles, model L3912, size #002.', 'The stitching is carried out by passing the thread through each hole with two needles, forming a traditional saddle stitch. Consistent thread tension helps maintain an even stitch pattern and a clean finish along the seam.'], ['Final edge burnishing', 'Once stitching is complete, if the edges still need it, extra sanding and touch-ups are done with sandpaper.', 'The edges then receive a final treatment with Tokonole and water. After the edges are prepared, a heated iron is used to burnish and refine the surface.', 'Columbus Leather Wax Stick may be applied as an additional finishing treatment, although this step is optional.'], ['Final stitch setting', 'As a final step, the stitched seams are gently tapped with a hammer to help the stitches settle into the leather, creating a flatter, more uniform appearance.']] }, calibNote: 'If the pattern does not come out 7 × 10 cm: set the fields on /editor back to the default numbers, measure both lines and enter their lengths (cm).' }, download: 'Download image', order: 'Order this one', measure: { toggle: 'Grid & measurements', width: 'Width', height: 'Height', thickness: 'Thickness', hole: 'Hole', spacing: 'Stitch spacing', inset: 'Stitching from edge' }, exitFull: 'Exit fullscreen', lockTop: 'Lock top view', hintPan: 'Drag to move, scroll to zoom', pattern: { btn: 'Download pattern', loading: 'Preparing…', title: 'Keyfob pattern', print: 'Print at 100% (actual size, not "fit to page"). Check the size against the calibration lines.', how: 'Cut it out, lay it on the leather, trace the outline and punch the holes straight through the paper.', cut: 'Cut line', holes: 'Holes: 3.85 mm pricking iron, slits at 45°', corner: 'Corner hole: pin it with an awl, start the iron from here', joint: 'Thread joint: 2 stitches sewn twice', ring: 'Ring hole', initials: 'Initials', stitches: 'Stitches', leather: 'Leather', thread: 'Thread', check: '10 mm', calibNote: 'If the pattern does not come out 3 × 8 cm: set the fields on /editor back to the default numbers, measure both lines and enter their lengths (cm).' }, calib: { label: 'Calibration', x: 'Horizontal line, cm', y: 'Vertical line, cm', hint: 'Leave the default numbers for the first print. Print, measure both rulers and enter what they come to (cm) - then every measurement prints true size.' } },
